 "unknowns": [
  {
   "configuration_id": "espec_tse_12_a_standard",
   "key": "temperature.range",
   "why": "The quote is verbatim: hot exposure 60 to 200 °C, cold exposure -65 to 0 °C. These are two separate ranges. The test area is only specified at -65..0 °C or +60..+200 °C, with nothing between 0 and 60 °C. Encoding one continuous range from 208.15 to 473.15 K would falsely match requests for steady temperatures like 25 or 40 °C. This needs two ranges or a cold-only/hot-only encoding."
  },
  {
   "configuration_id": "espec_tse_12_a_standard",
   "key": "optical_access",
   "why": "The Viewing window option is explicitly marked TSD. The catalog says 'Optional viewing window with interior lighting allows checking on specimens and wiring during testing. (TSD)'. The complete TSE-12-A specification table lists no window, so 'no optical access' for this model rests on the complete specification."
  },
  {
   "configuration_id": "espec_tse_12_a_standard",
   "key": "sample.height.max",
   "why": "148 mm is the raw inside height. The table also says 'Specimen baskets: Shelf brackets on 2 levels of fixed location', and the supplied baskets are W320×H35×D230 mm. With fixed shelf levels, the usable specimen height is well below 148 mm and is not stated, so 148 mm is not a conservative specimen-height limit."
  },
  {
   "configuration_id": "espec_tse_12_a_air_cooled",
   "key": "temperature.range",
   "why": "The catalog gives two disjoint exposure ranges: hot +60 to +200 C and cold -65 to 0 C. The two-zone TSE has no ambient zone, so nothing is documented between 0 and +60 C. The single range [-65, +200] C claims set points in that gap (e.g. 25 C) that are not documented. The NA page's '-65 to 200°C' is the same union. Store the cold and hot ranges separately or leave this key unknown."
  }
 ],
